.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KVM-CREATE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kvm-create
.Nd create a KVM virtual machine whose disk is a ZFS volume
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar name
.Op Fl -ram Ar MB
.Op Fl -cpus Ar N
.Op Fl -disk Ar GB
.Op Fl -iso Ar path
.Op Fl -bridge Ar interface
.Op Fl -os Ar variant
.Op Fl -volblocksize Ar size
.Op Fl -vnc-listen Ar address
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
creates a virtual machine called
.Ar name
whose disk is a ZFS volume,
.Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar name ,
rather than an image file.
The disk is then a dataset like any other: it can be snapshotted with
.Xr kvm-snap 8 ,
cloned with
.Xr kvm-clone 8 ,
and sent to another pool or machine with
.Xr zfs-send 8 .
.Pp
In order,
.Nm :
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
checks the name and every value, and changes nothing if any is wrong;
.It
creates the volume, sparse and compressed (compression is inherited from
.Pa rpool/vms ) ,
and waits up to three seconds for its device node;
.It
defines and starts the machine with
.Xr virt-install 1 :
UEFI firmware, the q35 machine type, the host's CPU model, a virtio disk
that passes the guest's discards through to the volume, a virtio network
interface on
.Ar interface ,
and a VNC console;
.It
if
.Xr virt-install 1
fails, destroys the volume it created;
.It
registers the machine in the kldload state database, so it appears in the
estate and in the Ansible inventory.
.El
.Pp
Without
.Fl -iso
the machine boots its disk, which is empty: it is ready to receive an image
(for example with
.Xr dd 1
or
.Xr zfs-receive 8
into the volume), or to boot from the network.
With
.Fl -iso
it boots the CD first and the disk after, which is an installation.
.Pp
The discard pass-through matters on a sparse volume: when the guest deletes
files and trims its filesystem, the volume gives the space back to the
pool, instead of keeping every block the guest ever wrote.
.Pp
A
.Ar name
is letters, digits,
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql _
and
.Ql - ,
up to 63 characters, starting with a letter or a digit.
It becomes both a dataset path and a libvirt domain name, so nothing else
is accepted: a
.Ql /
would create a nested dataset.
.Pp
.Nm
must run as root; run as a user it re-executes itself with
.Xr sudo 8 .
.Fl -help
needs neither.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--volblocksize size"
.It Fl -ram Ar MB
Memory in megabytes, 64 or more.
The default is 2048.
.It Fl -cpus Ar N
Virtual CPUs.
The default is 2.
.It Fl -disk Ar GB
The volume's size in gigabytes.
The volume is sparse, so this is the size the guest sees, not space taken
from the pool.
The default is 20.
.It Fl -iso Ar path
Attach
.Ar path
as a CD and boot it first.
The file must be readable by the hypervisor's
.Ql qemu
user, not only by root; see
.Sx DIAGNOSTICS .
.It Fl -bridge Ar interface
The bridge the machine's network interface joins.
The default is
.Ql br0 ,
the host's LAN bridge on a kldload install.
.Ql virbr0
is libvirt's NAT network.
.It Fl -os Ar variant
The operating system, as libvirt's osinfo database names it
.Po
.Ql osinfo-query os
lists them
.Pc ,
which lets
.Xr virt-install 1
choose devices and defaults for that system.
The default is
.Ql generic ,
which works for anything and makes
.Xr virt-install 1
print a warning.
.It Fl -volblocksize Ar size
The volume's block size, written like
.Ql 8K ,
.Ql 16K
or
.Ql 64K .
The default, 64K, suits sequential I/O, compression and clones; 8K or 16K
suits a guest running a database with small random writes.
It cannot be changed after the volume exists.
.It Fl -vnc-listen Ar address
The address the machine's VNC console listens on.
The default is
.Ql 0.0.0.0 ,
every interface, with no password; see
.Sx CAVEATS .
.Ql 127.0.0.1
keeps the console on this host, to be reached through an
.Xr ssh 1
tunnel.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
Needs no root and changes nothing.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /dev/zvol/rpool/vms/ Ns Ar name
The machine's disk.
.It Pa rpool/vms@pre-create- Ns Ar name
A snapshot of the parent dataset, taken before each create; the ten most
recent are kept.
It is not recursive and holds none of any machine's disk.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
The machine was created.
.It 1
The volume already exists, or creating the volume or the machine failed.
.It 2
A usage error: no name, a name that is not a plain name, a missing or bad
value, or an unknown flag.
Nothing was created.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Every output below was printed by
.Nm
on a kldload host on 2026-09-29.
The machines were throwaway ones named
.Ql mp-3173365-* ,
put on libvirt's NAT network with
.Fl -bridge Ar virbr0
so an empty disk never network-boots on the LAN, and deleted afterwards
with
.Xr kvm-delete 8 .
.Ss The defaults (--bridge)
2048 MB, 2 CPUs, a 20 GB volume:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-create mp-3173365-a --bridge virbr0
Creating ZFS zvol: rpool/vms/mp-3173365-a (20G, thin provisioned, volblocksize=64K)...
Creating VM: mp-3173365-a (2048MB RAM, 2 vCPUs, 20GB disk)...
WARNING  Using --osinfo generic, VM performance may suffer. Specify an accurate OS for optimal results.

Starting install...
Creating domain...                                          |         00:00
Domain creation completed.

VM 'mp-3173365-a' created
  Disk:    /dev/zvol/rpool/vms/mp-3173365-a
  Console: virsh console mp-3173365-a
  VNC:     virsh vncdisplay mp-3173365-a
  Snap:    kvm-snap mp-3173365-a
  Clone:   kvm-clone mp-3173365-a <new-name>
.Ed
.Pp
The volume is sparse and compressed (the second machine, below, was
asked about in the same command):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs get -H -o name,property,value \e
      volsize,volblocksize,compression,refreservation \e
      rpool/vms/mp-3173365-a rpool/vms/mp-3173365-b
rpool/vms/mp-3173365-a	volsize	20G
rpool/vms/mp-3173365-a	volblocksize	64K
rpool/vms/mp-3173365-a	compression	lz4
rpool/vms/mp-3173365-a	refreservation	none
\&...
.Ed
.Pp
Without
.Fl -bridge
the machine joins
.Ql br0 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-create web01
# on the LAN bridge; the host's DHCP server gives it an address
.Ed
.Ss Memory, CPUs and disk (--ram, --cpus, --disk)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-create mp-3173365-b --ram 4096 --cpus 4 --disk 40 --bridge virbr0
Creating ZFS zvol: rpool/vms/mp-3173365-b (40G, thin provisioned, volblocksize=64K)...
Creating VM: mp-3173365-b (4096MB RAM, 4 vCPUs, 40GB disk)...
\&...
VM 'mp-3173365-b' created
# virsh dominfo mp-3173365-b | grep -E 'State|Max memory|CPU\e(s\e)'
State:          running
CPU(s):         4
Max memory:     4194304 KiB
.Ed
.Ss A database guest (--volblocksize)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-create mp-3173365-c --disk 60 --volblocksize 16K --bridge virbr0
Creating ZFS zvol: rpool/vms/mp-3173365-c (60G, thin provisioned, volblocksize=16K)...
\&...
# zfs get -H -o name,value volblocksize rpool/vms/mp-3173365-c
rpool/vms/mp-3173365-c	16K
.Ed
.Ss Naming the operating system (--os)
No
.Ql generic
warning, and libvirt records the system:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-create mp-3173365-d --os debian13 --bridge virbr0
Creating ZFS zvol: rpool/vms/mp-3173365-d (20G, thin provisioned, volblocksize=64K)...
Creating VM: mp-3173365-d (2048MB RAM, 2 vCPUs, 20GB disk)...
\&...
# virsh dumpxml mp-3173365-d | grep -oE 'libosinfo:os id="[^"]*"'
libosinfo:os id="http://debian.org/debian/13"
.Ed
.Ss A console only this host can reach (--vnc-listen)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-create mp-3173365-e --vnc-listen 127.0.0.1 --bridge virbr0
\&...
# virsh dumpxml mp-3173365-e | grep -oE "graphics type='vnc'[^>]*listen='[^']*'"
graphics type='vnc' port='5904' autoport='yes' listen='127.0.0.1'
$ ssh -L 5904:127.0.0.1:5904 root@hypervisor
# then point a VNC viewer at localhost:5904
.Ed
.Ss Installing from a CD (--iso)
The image has to be where the hypervisor can read it;
.Pa /var/lib/libvirt/images
is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-create mp-3173365-f \e
      --iso /var/lib/libvirt/images/kvm-create-example.iso --bridge virbr0
Creating ZFS zvol: rpool/vms/mp-3173365-f (20G, thin provisioned, volblocksize=64K)...
Creating VM: mp-3173365-f (2048MB RAM, 2 vCPUs, 20GB disk)...
\&...
Domain is still running. Installation may be in progress.
You can reconnect to the console to complete the installation process.
VM 'mp-3173365-f' created
\&...
# virsh dumpxml mp-3173365-f | grep -oE "<source file='[^']*'|<boot dev='[^']*'" | head -4
<boot dev='cdrom'
<boot dev='hd'
<source file='/var/lib/libvirt/images/kvm-create-example.iso'
.Ed
.Ss Mistakes it refuses (nothing is created)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-create web/01
kvm-create: 'web/01' is not a plain name (letters, digits, . _ -)
# kvm-create probe-x --disk 0
kvm-create: --disk must be a positive number of GB (got: 0)
# kvm-create probe-x --ram 10
kvm-create: --ram must be a number of MB, 64 or more (got: 10)
# kvm-create probe-x --cpus two
kvm-create: --cpus must be a positive number (got: two)
# kvm-create probe-x --volblocksize 7Q
kvm-create: --volblocksize must look like 8K, 16K or 64K (got: 7Q)
# kvm-create probe-x --iso /nonexistent.iso
kvm-create: --iso /nonexistent.iso: no such readable file
# kvm-create probe-x --vnc-listen 'x;y'
kvm-create: --vnc-listen must be an address (got: x;y)
# kvm-create probe-x --ram
kvm-create: --ram needs a value
# kvm-create probe-x --foo
kvm-create: unknown option: --foo
# (each of the nine above exited 2)
# kvm-create mp-3173365-a --bridge virbr0
kvm-create: rpool/vms/mp-3173365-a already exists; pick another name or kvm-delete it first
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "kvm-create: NAME is not a plain name (letters, digits, . _ -)"
The name would not be a safe dataset path or domain name.
Exit 2.
.It "kvm-create: --FLAG must be ... (got: VALUE)"
A value is out of range or not a number; the message names the flag.
Exit 2.
.It "kvm-create: rpool/vms/NAME already exists; pick another name or kvm-delete it first"
A volume of that name exists, whether or not a machine uses it.
Exit 1.
.It "kvm-create: virt-install failed; destroying rpool/vms/NAME"
The machine could not be defined or started;
.Xr virt-install 1 Ns 's
own error is printed above this line, and the volume created for it is
destroyed.
Exit 1.
The commonest cause is an ISO the hypervisor cannot read.
On 2026-09-29 an image in a home directory gave:
.Bd -literal
WARNING  /home/anthony/.cache/kvm-create-example.iso may not be accessible by the hypervisor. You will need to grant the 'qemu' user search permissions for the following directories: ['/home/anthony/.cache']
\&...
ERROR    internal error: QEMU unexpectedly closed the monitor (vm='mp-3173365-f'): 2026-09-30T05:24:43.818227Z qemu-system-x86_64: -blockdev {"driver":"file","filename":"/home/anthony/.cache/kvm-create-example.iso","node-name":"libvirt-1-storage","read-only":true}: Could not open '/home/anthony/.cache/kvm-create-example.iso': Permission denied
\&...
kvm-create: virt-install failed; destroying rpool/vms/mp-3173365-f
.Ed
Move the image to
.Pa /var/lib/libvirt/images .
.It "kvm-create: could not destroy rpool/vms/NAME -- remove it by hand: zfs destroy rpool/vms/NAME"
The clean-up after a failed
.Xr virt-install 1
did not succeed; the volume is left behind and nothing uses it.
.It "kvm-create: WARNING: NAME is not in the kldload state DB (kldload-db vm-register failed)"
The machine exists and runs, but the estate and the Ansible inventory will
not show it until it is registered.
.It "kvm-create: /dev/zvol/rpool/vms/NAME did not appear after 3 seconds"
The volume was created but its device node never appeared; udev is slow or
stuck.
Exit 1, and the volume is left for inspection.
.El
